# kernel-usbnet.fragment — what the Dot's kernel additionally turns on for USB Ethernet on
# the micro-USB port. Applied by build-kernel.sh after kernel-bt.fragment; every other
# option stays as Amazon built it. The intended adapter is a Realtek RTL8152
# (0bda:8152, bcdDevice 0x0200). Hardware testing on 2026-10-29 proved that its
# advertised CDC-ECM configuration enumerates but cannot transmit on Biscuit's
# 3.18 MUSB path, so the in-kernel Realtek driver owns it instead.
#
# CONFIG_CMD_MODE_CHANGE enables Amazon's own forced-mode switch, /proc/mchange. With it,
# "echo host > /proc/mchange" runs MediaTek's full host-entry sequence without needing the
# micro-USB ID pin grounded (force_mode overrides the IDDIG read in musb_is_host()):
# host EP FIFO table, session clear, force PHY idle (0x6c/0x6d), session set, force PHY
# host (VBUSVALID/AVALID/BVALID/IDDIG=0), musb_start(), MUSB_HST_MODE(). "echo device >
# /proc/mchange" goes back to gadget mode (the serial console is the gadget, so entering
# host mode drops the console and it returns after switching back). VBUS must come from
# the cable: under CMD_MODE_CHANGE the driver deliberately does not drive its VBUS GPIO.
# Verified on-device 2026-10-25: host mode enumerates the adapter and device
# mode restores the serial console.
CONFIG_CMD_MODE_CHANGE=y
# usbcore announces every new USB device (descriptors, chosen config) in dmesg;
# Linux 3.18 keeps this code in Amazon's existing drivers/usb/core/hub.c.
CONFIG_USB_ANNOUNCE_NEW_DEVICES=y
# Keep generic CDC-ECM for genuinely class-compliant adapters. usbnet selects
# MII; CDCETHER depends on usbnet. The files are grafted from upstream
# linux-3.18.19 by build-kernel.sh (Amazon's tarball omits them). The upstream
# RTL8152 blacklist entry remains in cdc_ether so r8152 owns 0bda:8152 without
# a probe race. The ASIX net drivers must stay OFF: they "default y" and select
# PHYLIB, and Amazon's tree has no drivers/net/phy.
CONFIG_MII=y
CONFIG_USB_USBNET=y
CONFIG_USB_NET_CDCETHER=y
# The unit's config dump doesn't mention the usbnet family drivers at all (they were
# invisible with USB_USBNET unset in the running kernel), so a fresh olddefconfig
# applies their Kconfig defaults — several "default y"; only CDCETHER has sources in
# Amazon's tarball (usbnet.c/cdc_ether.c are grafted from upstream, see
# build-kernel.sh). Pin the whole family off (the CDCETHER line is this tree's
# symbol name; CONFIG_USB_NET_CDC_ETHER is the 4.x spelling). CDC_SUBSET's own
# sub-features (USB_ARMLINUX/USB_BELKIN/USB_EPSON2888) fold away with it.
# CONFIG_USB_NET_AX8817X is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_AX88179_178A is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_CDC_NCM is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_NET1080 is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_CDC_SUBSET is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_ZAURUS is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_PLUSB is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_MCS7830 is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_INT51X1 is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_DM9601 is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_RNDIS_HOST is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_CDC_EEM is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_SMSC75XX is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_SMSC95XX is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_SR9700 is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_SR9800 is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_CX82310_ETH is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_CDC_MBIM is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_QMI_WWAN is not set
# CONFIG_USB_NET_HUAWEI_CDC_NCM is not set
# Built in so the adapter is usable before the rootfs is selected and no loose
# kernel module has to match the unit-specific recovery kernel.
CONFIG_USB_RTL8152=y
# (GL6200A/KAWETH/SIERRA_NET and friends do not exist as symbols in Amazon's
# trimmed usb20 Kconfig; the fragment check proved that on the first build.)
